#This serverless web application is powered entirely by AWS services working together behind the scenes to deliver a fast, reliable, and cost-effective user experience.
#The website’s static files—things like HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and images—are stored in Amazon S3. S3 is perfect for this because it offers secure, durable storage without the need to maintain any servers.
#To make the site load quickly for users around the world, Amazon CloudFront sits in front of S3. CloudFront is a global Content Delivery Network (CDN) that caches content at edge locations close to users, which dramatically reduces load times and improves performance. When someone visits the app in their browser, their requests go through CloudFront first, and CloudFront serves the content or forwards the request to the backend if needed.
#For managing user sign-ups, logins, and authentication, the app relies on Amazon Cognito. Cognito issues secure JWT tokens once a user is logged in. These tokens are then checked by Amazon API Gateway, ensuring only authenticated users can access protected backend features.
#API Gateway acts as the front door for all API requests. When a request comes in, it triggers AWS Lambda functions that run the application’s logic. Lambda lets us run code without provisioning servers—each function handles a specific task such as creating or fetching data.
#All application data lives in Amazon DynamoDB, a fully managed NoSQL database. DynamoDB is designed for speed and scales automatically, which means we only pay for what we actually use—ideal for workloads that may grow or fluctuate over time.
#To keep an eye on how everything is running, Amazon CloudWatch collects logs, metrics, and performance data from Lambda and API Gateway. This makes it easier for developers to spot issues, improve performance, and understand how the system behaves in real time.
#By combining services like S3, CloudFront, Cognito, API Gateway, Lambda, DynamoDB, and CloudWatch, this architecture delivers a modern, fully serverless application. It avoids the hassle of managing servers, scales easily with demand, stays secure, and keeps costs low—all while providing a smooth experience for users.
##In the architecture, the S3 is connecetd to API Gateway, but that doesn't mean, it invoke API URL or send HTTP requets to API Gateway, It actually means , "S3 hosts the static frontend. The connection represents client-side JavaScript making HTTPS requests from the browser to API Gateway. S3 itself does not invoke the API.”
